          "model": "A nail is solid steel, with a density of about 8 g/cm³, so it is much denser than water and sinks. A ship is a hollow steel shell. What matters is the average density of the whole ship: its total mass divided by the total volume enclosed by its hull, including all the air inside. Because the hull encloses a huge volume of air, the ship’s average density is less than that of seawater (about 1.03 g/cm³), so it floats. If the hull is holed, water replaces the air: the ship’s mass increases while its volume stays the same, so its average density rises above that of seawater and it sinks. (In Year 10 you will also explain floating using the buoyancy force.)",
