        "deeper": {
          "q": "Nobody has ever seen inside an atom the way you see a chair. So how can scientists be confident that the nuclear (Rutherford–Bohr) model is right? Is it the \"true\" picture of an atom?",
          "zh": "没有人能像看椅子一样看到原子内部。那么科学家凭什么相信原子核模型（卢瑟福—玻尔模型）是对的？它就是原子的\"真实\"样子吗？",
          "hint": "Think about evidence, predictions, many scientists checking each other’s work — and the fact that models keep being improved.",
          "hintZh": "提示：想想证据、预测、许多科学家相互检验——以及模型一直在被改进这一事实。",
          "model": "Scientists are confident because the model is built on evidence. Experiments such as the gold-foil experiment gave results that the old model could not explain but the nuclear model could, and the model makes predictions that keep being confirmed — for example, the Bohr model explains why elements in the same group of the periodic table behave alike. Many scientists in different countries have tested it and reached a consensus. But it is not the \"true\" picture: it is a model. Electrons do not really travel in neat circular orbits like planets; senior science describes them as clouds of probability. So the Bohr model is useful and well supported, but it has limitations, and like all scientific ideas it can be refined when new evidence appears.",
          "modelZh": "科学家有信心，是因为这个模型建立在证据之上。金箔实验等实验得到的结果，旧模型解释不了，原子核模型却能解释；而且模型做出的预测不断被证实——例如玻尔模型能解释为什么周期表同一族的元素性质相似。许多国家的科学家都检验过它，并达成了共识（consensus）。但它并不是原子的\"真实\"样子，而是一个模型：电子并不像行星那样沿整齐的圆形轨道运动，高中科学会把电子描述成概率\"电子云\"。所以玻尔模型很有用、证据充分，但也有局限；和所有科学观点一样，出现新证据时它还会被修正。"
        },
