              "In 1869, the Russian chemist Dmitri Mendeleev arranged the 63 elements then known in order of atomic mass, putting elements with similar properties in the same column. Where the pattern did not fit, he left gaps and predicted the properties of elements not yet discovered. When germanium was found in 1886, it closely matched his predictions for the element he had called \"eka-silicon\". Other scientists then refined his table.",
              "Today there are 118 known elements, 94 of which occur naturally. They are arranged by **atomic number** into **groups** (columns) and **periods** (rows). **Metals** are on the left and in the centre and conduct heat and electricity; **non-metals** are on the right and generally do not."
