          "model": "Carbon dioxide dissolves in seawater and forms a weak acid, so the pH falls. Seawater at pH 8.1 is still alkaline, but it is becoming less so — this is called ocean acidification. Shellfish build their shells from calcium carbonate, which is a base. In less alkaline water it is harder for them to build and repair their shells, because the acid reacts with carbonate and less of it is available; young shellfish are especially at risk. This matters in New Zealand because green-lipped mussels are an important farmed export, and pāua are a valued taonga species and food. The change looks tiny, but each step of 1 on the pH scale is a ten-times change in acidity, so a fall of 0.1 means seawater is roughly a quarter more acidic than before.",
