        "deeper": {
          "q": "Robert Hooke saw cork cells in 1665. Today, electron microscopes can magnify more than a million times. How has better technology changed what we know about cells? Could there be cell parts that we have still not discovered?",
          "zh": "1665 年胡克看到了软木细胞；如今电子显微镜能放大一百万倍以上。技术进步怎样改变了我们对细胞的认识？会不会还有我们没有发现的细胞结构？",
          "hint": "Think about what each new kind of microscope revealed — first cells, then nuclei and chloroplasts, then the inside of mitochondria and viruses. What does that pattern suggest about the future?",
          "hintZh": "提示：想想每种新显微镜揭示了什么——先是细胞，然后是细胞核和叶绿体，再是线粒体内部和病毒。这个规律对未来意味着什么？",
          "model": "Each improvement in technology has revealed more. Hooke’s simple microscope showed that living things are built from cells. Better light microscopes and stains showed the nucleus, chloroplasts and other organelles, and led to the idea that all living things are made of cells. Electron microscopes, invented in the 1930s, magnify far more and revealed the folded inner membranes of mitochondria, tiny ribosomes and viruses, which are too small for any light microscope. Today, glowing (fluorescent) markers let scientists watch particular molecules moving inside living cells. Because every new tool has shown things nobody expected, it is very likely that future technology will reveal structures or processes we do not yet know about. Scientific knowledge is reliable, but it is always open to change when new evidence appears.",
          "modelZh": "每一次技术进步都让我们看到更多。胡克的简易显微镜显示生物是由细胞构成的；更好的光学显微镜和染色技术让人们看到了细胞核、叶绿体等细胞器，并由此提出\"一切生物都由细胞构成\"的观点。20 世纪 30 年代发明的电子显微镜放大倍数高得多，揭示了线粒体内部折叠的膜、微小的核糖体，以及光学显微镜根本看不到的病毒。如今，科学家用发光（荧光）标记观察活细胞里特定分子的运动。既然每一种新工具都带来了意想不到的发现，未来的技术很可能还会揭示我们现在还不知道的结构或过程。科学知识是可靠的，但当新证据出现时，它总是可以被修正。"
        },
