              "Carbon dioxide diffuses into the leaf through tiny pores called **stomata**, and water travels up from the roots. Leaves are thin, so gases have only a short distance to diffuse, and broad, to catch plenty of light. The plant uses the glucose for respiration, stores it as starch, or builds it into cellulose for new cell walls. So a kauri’s huge mass was built mainly from carbon dioxide taken from the air!"
