        "deeper": {
          "q": "If you threw a ball in deep space, far from any planet or star, what would happen to it, and why? Why does a ball rolling across the floor on Earth always stop?",
          "zh": "如果在远离任何行星和恒星的太空深处扔出一个球，它会怎样？为什么？为什么在地球上滚过地板的球总会停下来？",
          "hint": "What forces act on the ball in deep space? What forces act on a ball rolling across a floor on Earth?",
          "hintZh": "提示：在太空深处，球受到哪些力？在地球上滚动的球受到哪些力？",
          "model": "In deep space, far from any planet or star, there is almost no gravity, no air and nothing for the ball to rub against, so once it leaves your hand the net force on it is (almost) zero. With no unbalanced force, an object keeps moving at a constant speed in a straight line, so the ball would carry on in the same direction at the same speed for an extremely long time. On Earth, a rolling ball has forces acting against its motion — friction with the floor and air resistance — so the net force points backwards and the ball slows down until it stops. The ball stops because a force acts on it, not because it \"runs out\" of motion. This was one of Isaac Newton’s big ideas: you need a force to change motion, not to keep it going.",
          "modelZh": "在远离行星和恒星的太空深处，几乎没有重力、没有空气，也没有东西和球摩擦，所以球离手后受到的合力（几乎）为零。没有非平衡力时，物体会保持匀速直线运动，所以球会沿同一方向、以同样的速度飞行极其漫长的时间。在地球上，滚动的球受到阻碍运动的力——与地板的摩擦力和空气阻力——合力方向与运动方向相反，所以球越滚越慢，最后停下。球停下是因为有力作用在它上面，而不是因为它的运动\"用完了\"。这正是牛顿的重要思想之一：改变运动需要力，维持运动并不需要力。"
        },
